{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"First, we note that $y=-7$ makes the denominator zero and so cannot be a solution.\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $y+7$ to get rid of the quotient. ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{2y - 4}{y + 7} \u0026= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\\\\\\\\n(y+7)\\cdot \\dfrac{2y - 4}{y + 7} \u0026= (y+7)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\\\\\\\\n2y-4\u0026=\\dfrac13 y+\\dfrac73\n\\\\\\\\\n\\dfrac53 y\u0026=\\dfrac{19}{3}\n\\\\\\\\\ny\u0026=\\dfrac{19}{5}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We get that $y=\\dfrac{19}{5}$ is a potential solution. It doesn't make the denominator zero, so it is indeed a solution.\n\n**In conclusion, $y=\\dfrac{19}{5}$**","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Solve for $y$.**\n\n$\\dfrac{2y - 4}{y + 7} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n$y =$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"answerForms":["proper","improper"],"maxError":0,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":3.8}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
